WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.680
How can one cell hold a lysosome at pH 4.7 and a mitochondrion near 8, in one

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.400
cytosol? Why does each settle at its own number?

3
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:18.470
This is the cell's pH ladder, with typical values from different cells.

4
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.450
Each marker is one compartment.

5
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.530
The white line is the cytosol.

6
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.370
Acid compartments sit below.

7
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.770
The matrix, the mitochondrion's inside, sits above.

8
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:34.043
Start with the wall.

9
00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:38.323
A membrane lets ions through slowly, but not never.

10
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:41.763
Protons trickle back toward the cytosol.

11
00:00:42.817 --> 00:00:46.537
Picture the lysosome as a container with a tap and a hole.

12
00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:53.417
The tap is a proton pump, the V-ATPase, spending ATP to push protons in.

13
00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:01.230
As protons pile up, more leak back out through the hole.

14
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.910
At some level, out matches in, and the level stops rising.

15
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.810
This picture needs no sensor to set that level.

16
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.390
That is the idea, for this acid lumen.

17
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.190
The level sits where pumping in meets leaking out.

18
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.550
Other things equal, a stronger pump makes it more acid.

19
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.670
A leakier wall makes it less acid.

20
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:30.123
Now a drug, bafilomycin, clamps the V-ATPase tap shut.

21
00:01:31.503 --> 00:01:36.603
Watch the ladder. Which way will the lysosome's marker move, up or down?

22
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:44.317
Up, toward the line: less acid.

23
00:01:45.477 --> 00:01:47.497
With the tap off, the hole keeps draining.

24
00:01:48.877 --> 00:01:52.317
In HeLa cells, bafilomycin made the Golgi less acid.

25
00:01:53.497 --> 00:01:55.397
So that acid is actively kept.

26
00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:01.750
The Golgi uses the same pump family, yet it settles higher, near six point six.

27
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.810
Does the lysosome have more pumps, a tighter wall, or something else?

28
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.550
That is still being worked out.

29
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:12.173
The nucleus is different.

30
00:02:13.453 --> 00:02:18.713
Its envelope is full of pores, and small ions, protons included, pass through them

31
00:02:18.713 --> 00:02:22.673
quickly. So its pH stays close to the cytosol's.

32
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:26.553
The ER is also a nonequilibrium store.

33
00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:33.113
A calcium pump called SERCA fills it to thousands of times the cytosol's level,

34
00:02:33.413 --> 00:02:36.633
while leaks and release channels move calcium out.

35
00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:41.213
Thapsigargin, a plant compound, blocks SERCA.

36
00:02:42.653 --> 00:02:45.353
Calcium keeps leaking out, and nothing pumps it back.

37
00:02:46.553 --> 00:02:50.113
The store runs down, and cytosol calcium rises for a while.

38
00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:53.753
The mitochondrion's pumps face the other way.

39
00:02:54.853 --> 00:02:58.353
They are the respiratory chain, driven by electron transport.

40
00:02:59.693 --> 00:03:04.273
While it runs, they push protons out of the matrix, so the matrix sits above the

41
00:03:04.273 --> 00:03:05.273
line.

42
00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:06.973
Why the other way?

43
00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:13.193
The matrix was once a bacterium's cytoplasm, and during respiration its

44
00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:16.193
electron-transport chain pumped protons outward.

45
00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.927
Chloroplasts did the same.

46
00:03:20.227 --> 00:03:26.107
Their stroma, the fluid inside, was once a cyanobacterium's cytoplasm, and in light

47
00:03:26.227 --> 00:03:27.227
it turns alkaline.

48
00:03:28.587 --> 00:03:31.607
Every proton pump here pushes out of a cytoplasm.

49
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:38.130
The acid lumens, the matrix and the stroma need ongoing energy-dependent transport.

50
00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.590
Acid lumens need their V-ATPase.

51
00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.670
The matrix and stroma need respiration or light.

52
00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.590
Stop that input, and those gradients run down.
